From: Aspartame GTK4 migration
Subject: [PATCH] casilda: send modifier state before the key it modifies

casilda_compositor_seat_key_notify() read the current modifier mask, sent
the key event, and only then sent the modifier update. The mask it reads is
the state the key event is modified by, so applying it afterwards put every
modifier one keystroke late: typing Shift+a produced 'a' and the next plain
key arrived shifted ('aB' instead of 'Ab'). Shift+Tab likewise reached the
client as a plain Tab, so reverse focus traversal did nothing.

Sending the modifiers first makes Shift, and therefore capitals and
ISO_Left_Tab, apply to the key they belong to.

Upstream candidate: https://gitlab.gnome.org/jpu/casilda

---
 src/casilda-compositor.c | 13 +++++++------
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

--- a/src/casilda-compositor.c
+++ b/src/casilda-compositor.c
@@ -1747,16 +1747,17 @@ casilda_compositor_seat_key_notify (GtkEventControllerKey    *self,
    * and we do not use EventControllerKey::modifiers signal because it changes after the mod key release
    */

-  /* Send key event first */
-  wlr_seat_keyboard_notify_key (priv->seat, time_msec, key - 8, state);
-
-
-  /* Update modifiers if necessary after */
+  /* Update modifiers first: the mask read above is the state this key event
+   * is modified by, so sending the key before it applied the modifier one
+   * keystroke late - Shift+a arrived as 'a' and the following plain key
+   * arrived shifted. */
   if (priv->keyboard.modifiers.depressed != depressed)
     {
       priv->keyboard.modifiers.depressed = depressed;
       wlr_seat_keyboard_send_modifiers (priv->seat, &priv->keyboard.modifiers);
     }
+
+  wlr_seat_keyboard_notify_key (priv->seat, time_msec, key - 8, state);
 }

 static gboolean
